room prop_7
kind geometry
import Newton, Principia, Andrew Motte trans., 1729 (Wikisource); Book I, Section II, Proposition VII; Plate 3, Figure 3.
caption If a body revolves in the circumference of a circle, find the law of centripetal force directed to any given point.
final 3
ceiling eq0 :: F \propto \dfrac{1}{SP^2}\cdot\dfrac{QR}{QT^2}
ceiling eq1 :: \angle VPA = 90^\circ \;\Rightarrow\; \triangle ZQR \sim \triangle ZTP \sim \triangle ZPA
ceiling eq2 :: QR\cdot L : QT^2 = AV^2 : PV^2 \;\Rightarrow\; F \propto \dfrac{1}{SP^2}\cdot\dfrac{1}{PV^3}

station 1
  gloss The circle is the orbit and S is a force centre placed anywhere inside it, not just the centre.
  color circblue #1E6FE0
  color centerorange #E8770A
  color radgreen #00A35A
  panel
    point O @(0,0)
    circle_cr circ O radiusval 3.2 color=circblue label=$VQPA$ at=W
    point P @(2.8,-1.5) label=$P$ at=SE
    point Q @(1.3,2.9) label=$Q$ at=NW
    point S @(0.7,-0.8) color=centerorange label=$S$ at=NE marker=dot
    segment SP S P color=radgreen heart label=$SP$ at=S
  text
    Proposition VII is the first concrete application of the force-measure proved in {radgreen|Proposition VI}, \
    now applied to the simplest curvilinear orbit: the {circblue|circle $VQPA$}. \
    A body $P$ revolves in the circumference and $Q$ is the next place into which it moves. \
    The remarkable freedom here is that the {centerorange|force centre $S$} need not sit at the centre $O$ — \
    it may lie anywhere inside the circle, and we still seek the law along the {radgreen|radius $SP$}, \
    which is the distance whose powers Prop. VI taught us to track.

station 2
  gloss The tangent, the chord PV through S, and the diameter VA seed the right angle at P.
  color circblue #1E6FE0
  color tanteal #00897B
  color diampurple #8E24AA
  panel
    point O @(0,0)
    circle_cr circ O radiusval 3.2 color=circblue
    point P @(2.8,-1.5) label=$P$ at=SE
    point V @(-1.65,0.3) label=$V$ at=W
    point A @(1.65,-0.3) label=$A$ at=E
    point S @(0.7,-0.8) label=$S$ at=NE marker=dot
    tangent_at tanpz on circ at P color=tanteal heart label=$PRZ$ at=NE
    segment PV P V color=diampurple label=$PV$ at=N
    segment VA V A color=diampurple label=$VA$ at=S
    segment AP A P color=diampurple label=$AP$ at=E
    angle VPA V P A
  text
    At the body's place we draw the {tanteal|tangent $PRZ$}, the forceless straight path the body would keep \
    without any centripetal pull — this is the heart of the deviation argument. \
    Through the force centre we draw the {diampurple|chord $PV$}, and through $V$ the {diampurple|diameter $VA$}, joining $AP$. \
    Because $VA$ is a diameter, the angle $VPA$ inscribed in the semicircle is a right angle, \
    and this single fact seeds the chain of similar triangles that will follow. \
    The {diampurple|chord $PV$ through $S$} is the intrinsic length that will ultimately govern the whole force law.

station 3
  gloss The QT and LR construction collapses QT squared over QR into PV cubed, giving the force law.
  color circblue #1E6FE0
  color tanteal #00897B
  color constred #D81B60
  panel
    point O @(0,0)
    circle_cr circ O radiusval 3.2 color=circblue
    point P @(2.8,-1.5) label=$P$ at=SE
    point Q @(1.3,2.9) label=$Q$ at=NW
    point S @(0.7,-0.8) label=$S$ at=NE marker=dot
    point V @(-1.65,0.3) label=$V$ at=W
    tangent_at tanpz on circ at P color=tanteal
    segment SP S P
    foot T from Q to S P
    perp QT through Q to S P color=constred heart label=$QT$ at=E
    parallel LR through Q to S P color=constred heart label=$LR$ at=N
    point R @(2.35,2.15) label=$R$ at=NE marker=dot
    point L @(-2.9,1.35) label=$L$ at=W marker=dot
    point Z @(3.5,-1.0) label=$Z$ at=E marker=dot
  text
    Now we lay down Prop. VI's apparatus: {constred|$QT$ perpendicular to $SP$} and {constred|$LR$ parallel to $SP$}, \
    meeting the tangent at $R$ and $Z$ and the circle at $L$. \
    The similar triangles $ZQR$, $ZTP$, $ZPA$ give $RP^2$ (that is $QR\cdot L$) to $QT^2$ as $AV^2$ to $PV^2$, \
    so multiplying by $SP^2/QR$ and letting $Q$ coincide with $P$ collapses the {constred|construction} into the clean chord-cube. \
    Substituting into Prop. VI yields $F \propto \dfrac{1}{SP^2\cdot PV^3}$; and in the special case where $S$ sits at $O$, \
    the chord $PV$ becomes the diameter and the law reduces to the pure inverse-square $1/SP^2$. \textit{Q.E.D.}
